>     The monster version of the spell is called "touch of death",
> and, as the name implies, it's a melee-range touch attack rather than a
> ray, so reflection does nothing, but magic resistance protects
> completely. The player spell is "finger of death", and it shoots a death
> ray out to a range of several squares (and you can hit yourself with it
> by bouncing it off a wall or a reflective monster). Both reflection and
> magic resistance protect against it, and reflection will bounce the ray
> back at its source. And both players and monsters can use wands of
> death, which work just like finger of death and can be defended against
> in the same ways.
> 
Another thing to note is that neither reflection nor magic resistance 
will protect against self zapped spells of Finger of Death or wands of 
death zapped at self ('.' at a direction prompt).  Unless you're poly'ed 
into an undead, that will lead to DYWYPI.

Oddly enough force bolt still 'boing!'s with MR.  (Reflection doesn't 
stop striking/force bolt)

